\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 For this lab I learned how containerization actually works by manually creating isolated environments. Because my Mac does not natively support Linux kernel namespaces, I used Multipass to set up an Ubuntu virtual machine as my host.\
In the first part of the lab, I created a UTS namespace to isolate the system hostname. I opened two separate shells and detached one from the host. When I changed the name of the isolated machine, the original host machine kept its normal name. This showed that the two environments were completely separated.\
Next, I worked on process isolation by creating a PID namespace. I made sure to fork the process and mount a fresh proc file system so the tools would read the data correctly. After starting a sleep process in the background, I checked the active processes. Instead of seeing everything running on the entire computer, I only saw the exact commands I had just started within that specific shell.\
Finally, I created a mount namespace to separate the file system. I mounted a temporary file directory and created a blank text file inside it. When I searched for that file inside the isolated environment, it showed up perfectly. However, when I checked that exact same folder path on the main host machine, the folder was completely empty.\
